A **function** is a reusable block of code that runs when you call it.

### Declaring a function
```js
function greet(name) {
  return "Hello, " + name + "!";
}

console.log(greet("Aarav"));  // Hello, Aarav!
```
- `name` is a **parameter** (an input)
- `return` sends a value back to the caller

### Arrow functions (modern style)
```js
const greet = (name) => {
  return "Hello, " + name + "!";
};

// Even shorter when there's a single expression:
const greet = (name) => `Hello, ${name}!`;
```
Arrow functions are the style you'll see in most modern code.

### Functions with no return
A function without `return` returns `undefined`:
```js
function logMessage(msg) {
  console.log(msg);
}
```

### Default parameters
```js
function greet(name = "there") {
  return `Hello, ${name}!`;
}
greet();          // Hello, there!
greet("Priya");  // Hello, Priya!
```

### Passing & using results
```js
function add(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}
const total = add(3, 4);   // 7
```
